    <body>
      <sec id="article-90190.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Restrictive lung disease encompasses a heterogeneous group of disorders characterized by reduced lung volumes and impaired compliance, resulting in compromised gas exchange and increased work of breathing. Intrinsic causes, such as interstitial lung diseases, involve fibrotic or inflammatory processes within the lung parenchyma. In contrast, extrinsic causes arise from factors outside the lungs, including obesity, neuromuscular disorders, chest wall abnormalities, and pleural pathology. Common clinical manifestations include progressive exertional dyspnea, reduced exercise tolerance, and chronic cough, with advanced disease leading to hypoxemia, respiratory failure, pulmonary hypertension, and cor pulmonale. Diagnosis is established primarily through pulmonary function testing, demonstrating decreased total lung capacity with a preserved or increased forced expiratory volume in 1 second to forced vital capacity ratio. High-resolution imaging, histopathology, and genetic testing further support diagnosis and classification. Prognosis varies widely by etiology, ranging from reversible conditions such as pleural effusion to progressive fibrotic diseases such as idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, which carries a median survival of 3 to 5 years.</p>
        <p>Participants completing this activity gain a comprehensive understanding of the diagnostic evaluation, prognosis, and management strategies for restrictive lung disease, emphasizing evidence-based application of antifibrotic therapy, immunosuppression, pulmonary rehabilitation, and supportive care. Clinicians enhance competence in distinguishing between intrinsic and extrinsic causes and tailoring management to the underlying pathophysiology. Collaboration with an interprofessional team, including pulmonologists, primary care clinicians, nurses, pharmacists, cardiologists, nutritionists, neurologists, and palliative care specialists, optimizes patient outcomes through coordinated care. Interdisciplinary involvement ensures the timely recognition of complications, the appropriate initiation of advanced therapies, and an improved quality of life for patients with progressive or terminal disease. By integrating knowledge across specialties, clinicians strengthen decision-making and deliver patient-centered care that improves long-term outcomes in restrictive lung disease.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Differentiate intrinsic from extrinsic causes of restrictive lung disease based on clinical presentation, pulmonary function testing, and imaging findings to improve diagnostic accuracy.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Assess disease severity and prognosis in patients with interstitial lung diseases, including idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, to guide timely initiation of antifibrotic therapy or referral for advanced care.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Implement evidence-based management strategies for extrinsic restrictive disorders, including obesity, neuromuscular disease, and chest wall abnormalities, in collaboration with an interprofessional healthcare team.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply principles of interprofessional collaboration by engaging pulmonologists, primary care clinicians, pharmacists, nutritionists, neurologists, cardiologists, and palliative care specialists to optimize long-term outcomes and quality of life in patients with restrictive lung disease.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=90190&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=90190">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Restrictive lung diseases are a heterogeneous set of pulmonary disorders defined by restrictive patterns on spirometry. These disorders are characterized by&#x000a0;decreased distensibility of the lungs, which compromises lung expansion and results in reduced lung volumes, particularly a reduction in total lung capacity (TLC).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;These functional characteristics differentiate them from obstructive pulmonary diseases, such as chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, bronchiectasis, asthma, and bronchiolitis, which are characterized by increased resistance to flow&#x000a0;due to partial or complete obstruction from the trachea to the terminal bronchioles. Restrictive disease accounts for approximately one-fifth of pulmonary disease, of which obstructive&#x000a0;disease is the majority (80%).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r2">[2]</xref></p>
        <p>Restrictive lung diseases&#x000a0;may be caused by the destruction of distal lung parenchyma due to infiltrates from inflammation, toxins, and mechanisms not yet fully understood (intrinsic causes) and extraparenchymal conditions (extrinsic causes). Within this group are diseases characterized by inflammatory changes involving&#x000a0;the alveolar interstitium, with possible involvement of the&#x000a0;peripheral bronchial structures.&#x000a0;The conditions leading to this destruction are the interstitial lung diseases (ILDs).&#x000a0;Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/23648">Interstitial Lung Disease</ext-link>," for further information.&#x000a0;This term refers to an umbrella of disorders characterized by diffuse cellular infiltrates in a periacinar distribution, encompassing a range of clinical presentations from occasional, self-limited inflammatory processes to severe, debilitating lung fibrosis. Other conditions originate from within the alveolus (edema, hemorrhage) and spread to interstitial structures. Whether the process begins in the interstitium or the alveolus, alteration of the lung architecture results in functional impairment.</p>
        <p>Conversely,&#x000a0;restrictive lung diseases may also result from disorders causing respiratory muscle dysfunction or chest wall restriction,&#x000a0;physically impeding inspiration. Neurological or neuromuscular diseases can limit the activity of the respiratory muscles, whereas skeletal abnormalities of the thorax, abdominal distension, and pleural space abnormalities can restrict lung expansion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Regardless of the intrinsic or extrinsic mechanisms, these diseases are characterized by impaired ventilatory function and respiratory failure.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>The&#x000a0;acronym "PAINT" categorizes the causes of restrictive lung disease into pleural, alveolar, interstitial, neuromuscular, and thoracic cage abnormalities.&#x000a0;A more precise classification&#x000a0;is based on the pathogenetic mechanism. Restrictive lung syndromes can be caused by:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary parenchyma diseases (intrinsic causes)</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Extrapulmonary diseases (extrinsic causes)</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Intrinsic causes involve the lung parenchyma, while the extrinsic restrictive lung diseases originate from neuromuscular disorders, obesity, and other extraparenchymal disorders.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;In intrinsic and extrinsic pulmonary conditions, lung volumes are reduced due to restrictions in&#x000a0;pulmonary mechanics.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Pulmonary Parenchyma Diseases (Intrinsic Causes)</bold>
</p>
        <p>Intrinsic restriction results from inflammatory or fibrotic processes within the lung tissue, most commonly ILDs. The etiologies include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Idiopathic
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis (IPF). Please see StatPearls' companion reference, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/28027">Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis</ext-link>," for more information.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP). Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/25967">Nonspecific Interstitial Pneumonia</ext-link>," for more information.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Acute interstitial pneumonia. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/22510">Acute Interstitial Pneumonia</ext-link>," for more information.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Exposure
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Inorganic dust exposure: Silicosis, asbestosis, talcosis, pneumoconiosis, berylliosis, hard metal fibrosis, coal workers' pneumoconiosis, chemical&#x000a0;pneumonitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Organic dust exposure: Farmer lung, bird fancier's lung, bagassosis, mushroom worker lung, humidifier lung, and hot-tub pneumonitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Medications: Nitrofurantoin, amiodarone, gold, phenytoin, thiazides, hydralazine, bleomycin, carmustine, cyclophosphamide, and methotrexate</p></list-item><list-item><p>Radiation therapy</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Autoimmune disease&#x02013;related interstitial lung disease. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/19709">Collagen Vascular Disease with Interstitial Lung</ext-link>," for more information.
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Systemic sclerosis&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Rheumatoid arthritis&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Ankylosing spondylitis&#x000a0;</p></list-item><list-item><p>Inflammatory myopathy&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Granulomatous disorders
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Sarcoidosis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP)</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Miscellaneous
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Pulmonary vasculitis</p></list-item><list-item><p>Pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis (formerly histiocytosis X)</p></list-item><list-item><p>Postviral pneumonitis, fibrosis from COVID-19&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r4">[4]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Smoking and air pollution are strong risk factors for developing ILDs like IPF.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r6">[6]</xref>&#x000a0;ILD may be grouped into diseases that lead to an&#x000a0;increased elastic recoil (diffuse infiltrative pneumopathies and interstitial diseases) and those with alveolar filling (pneumonia). Several ILDs demonstrate strong genetic associations. For example, the <italic toggle="yes">MUC5B</italic> promoter gene and variants of telomere maintenance genes are risk factors for IPF, fibrotic hypersensitivity pneumonitis, and rheumatoid arthritis&#x02013;related ILD.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r8">[8]</xref> Similarly, familial aggregation is seen in sarcoidosis with approximately a 4-fold relative risk.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r9">[9]</xref> Important genetic variations associated with the risk of ILDs and different phenotypes are listed in <bold>Table 1 </bold>below<bold>.</bold><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r11">[11]</xref></p>
        <table-wrap id="article-90190.table0" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 1. Genes and Phenotypes of ILDs</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="height: 0px; width: 800px;" border="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">ILD/phenotype</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Gene associated</td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 13px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Comment</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 39px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">IPF/Familial interstitial pneumonia</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic toggle="yes">MUC5B </italic>promoter (<italic toggle="yes">rs35705950)</italic>&#x000a0;telomere genes (<italic toggle="yes">TERT, TERC, RTEL1, PARN, DKC1, TINF2, ACD</italic>); surfactant genes (<italic toggle="yes">SFTPC, SFTPA1/2, ABCA3</italic>)</td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 39px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP) pattern, family history, and associated liver disease</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
