              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 26px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Rheumatoid arthritis&#x02013;associated ILD</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<italic toggle="yes">MUC5B (rs35705950)</italic>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 26px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">UIP pattern</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 14px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 14px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sarcoidosis</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 14px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">HLA class II <italic toggle="yes">(HLA-DRB1, HLA-DQB1), BTNL2, ANXA11</italic></td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 14px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Influence susceptibility and chronicity</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 3px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 3px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Langerhans cell histiocytosis</p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 3px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Somatic <italic toggle="yes">BRAF</italic> V600E, <italic toggle="yes">MAP2K1&#x000a0;</italic></p>
</td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 3px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic toggle="yes">MAPK</italic> pathway mutations have implications for targeted therapy</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 5px;">
                <td style="width: 246.969px; height: 5px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Lymphangioleiomyomatosis&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="width: 661.344px; height: 5px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">TSC1,TSC2</italic></td>
                <td style="width: 270.688px; height: 5px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><italic toggle="yes">mTOR</italic> pathway activation; responds to sirolimus, everolimus</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <p>
<bold>Extrinsic or Extrapulmonary&#x000a0;Diseases</bold>
</p>
        <p>Restrictive lung disorders may arise from the dysfunction of the ventilatory pump, including the respiratory centers in the brain, the respiratory muscles (diaphragm, intercostal muscles), the motor neurons, or external mechanical constraints on lung expansion. These etiologies can be broadly classified into:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Neuromuscular disorders:&#x000a0;</bold>Impaired respiratory muscle function results in low vital capacity and TLC. Examples include muscular dystrophy, myasthenia gravis, amyotrophic lateral sclerosis, poliomyelitis, and phrenic nerve palsy/neuropathies.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Chest wall/skeletal abnormalities:</bold> Deformities or rigidity of the thoracic cage can restrict chest expansion, reducing TLC, as seen in kyphoscoliosis, pectus excavatum (depressed sternum), costosternal or costovertebral fusion, ankylosing spondylitis, and extensive burns/scar contractures.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Pleural diseases:</bold> Conditions affecting the pleura can mechanically impede lung inflation and reduce TLC, such as pleural effusions, trapped lung, pleural fibrosis/scarring, extensive plaques, chronic empyema, asbestosis, and mesothelioma.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Abdominal factors:</bold> Increased intra-abdominal pressure can hinder diaphragmatic movement, as observed in cases of massive ascites and large abdominal tumors.</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Obesity:</bold> Morbid obesity can mechanically restrict chest wall and diaphragmatic movement,&#x000a0;reduce lung volumes, cause premature airway closure, leading to the development of intrinsic positive end-expiratory pressure, increased work of breathing, and obesity hypoventilation syndrome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r13">[13]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Estimating the prevalence of restrictive diseases with precision is challenging due to their diverse range of pathological conditions, each of which can present at various clinical stages.&#x000a0;However, intrinsic lung diseases affect between 3 and 6 per 100,000 persons in the United States.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;The prevalence of sarcoidosis in North&#x000a0;America has been estimated at 10 to 40 per 100,000 persons,&#x000a0;with a higher prevalence in&#x000a0;Sweden (64 per 100,000 persons).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r15">[15]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from national and global population studies have demonstrated that certain&#x000a0;groups are at a higher risk of having a restrictive pattern on spirometry. The populations at higher&#x000a0;risk include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Older adults: The prevalence of restrictive conditions increases from 2.7 per 100,000 persons in individuals aged 35 to 44 to more than 175 per 100,000 in those older than 75.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;Sarcoidosis, connective tissue&#x02013;associated diseases, and pulmonary Langerhans cell histiocytosis occur more frequently in patients aged 20 to 40.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Black individuals:&#x000a0;Compared with White individuals (10.9 per 100,000), the prevalence in this group is 35.5 per 100,000 persons.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Women: Sarcoidosis is slightly more common in&#x000a0;women. Moreover,&#x000a0;women have an increased risk of a restrictive pattern.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;However, IPF is more common in men.&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Obese individuals: Restrictive patterns are related to an elevated body mass index, with decreased lung volumes attributed to increased central adiposity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Smoking: Individuals&#x000a0;with IPF are usually current or past tobacco smokers.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r12">[12]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Those who have specific occupational and environmental exposures are also at increased risk. Frequently encountering substances at workplaces such as asbestos, coal dust, and several other hazardous dusts can cause lung tissue alterations, leading to inflammation, tissue scarring, and a restrictive pattern on spirometry.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r17">[17]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>Restrictive lung diseases are rare during pregnancy, but if present,&#x000a0;they are usually&#x000a0;due to neuromuscular disease or kyphoscoliosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;Results from an epidemiologic study showed that restrictive patterns on spirometry decreased from 7.2% to 5.4% between 1988 and 1994 and 2007 and 2010, which may be related to improved occupational safety precautions and declining cigarette smoking rates.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r12">[12]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>Intrinsic pulmonary restriction&#x000a0;is most commonly characterized by inflammation of the pulmonary parenchyma, followed by collagen deposition in the interstitium, ultimately leading to pulmonary fibrosis. Over time, this progressive fibrosis thickens the alveolar septa, imposing a physical barrier to gas exchange. On pulmonary function testing (PFT), decreased diffusing capacity for carbon monoxide (DLCO)&#x000a0;is observed. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/20633">Diffusing Capacity of the Lungs for Carbon Monoxide</ext-link>," for more information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Besides decreasing DLCO, lung tissue fibrosis decreases&#x000a0;the compliance of the lung tissue, reducing inspiratory capacity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;The pathophysiology of extrapulmonary disease is due to altered mechanical function due&#x000a0;to rib cage deformity, neurological or muscular pathologies, or pleural effusions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Compliance</bold>
</p>
        <p>The distensibility of the respiratory system is termed compliance (C). Compliance represents the volume change produced by a change in the distending pressure, determined by the elastic forces formula:</p>
        <p>
<italic toggle="yes">C= &#x00394;V/&#x00394;P</italic>
</p>
        <p>Notably, lung compliance is independent of the thoracic cage, a semirigid casing, but the lung and thorax are systems arranged in series.&#x000a0;Therefore, the compliance of an intact respiratory system is the sum of the compliances of both structures. Disease of the lungs, pleura, or chest wall affects compliance.&#x000a0;Lung&#x000a0;compliance decreases if the lung is overly rigid (in restrictive pathologies) and increases in&#x000a0;less stiff conditions, such as emphysema.&#x000a0;Thoracic&#x000a0;compliance decreases with reduced distensibility of the&#x000a0;rib cage (obesity, kyphoscoliosis).</p>
        <p>The combined compliance of the thorax and lungs is 110 mL/cm H<sub>2</sub>O, whereas the&#x000a0;compliance alone is approximately 200 mL/cm H<sub>2</sub>O.&#x000a0;Decreased compliance from intrapulmonary or extrapulmonary causes results in greater pressure generation and energy requirements from the respiratory muscles for any given tidal volume.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;These&#x000a0;changes in respiratory physiology eventually lead to tissue hypoxia and dyspnea.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hypoxemia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hypoxemia (PaO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>&#x0003c;60 mm Hg), with or without hypercapnia (PaCO<sub>2&#x000a0;</sub>&#x0003e;45 mm Hg), can be a consequence of lung damage and an expression of respiratory failure. In restrictive diseases, hypoxia occurs through different mechanisms depending on the type of disease. In intrinsic conditions, alterations in the gas diffusion with a ventilation-perfusion ratio mismatch contribute to hypoxia. This process leads to an increased intrapulmonary shunt, defined as the portion of the pulmonary blood flow that bypasses the alveoli or perfuses unventilated alveoli and does not participate in gas exchange. Conversely, hypoventilation and respiratory pump failure are prominent in extrinsic disease.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Functional Characteristics </bold>
</p>
        <p>Functional characteristics of a restrictive pattern on PFTs include decreased TLC and forced vital capacity (FVC). Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/161798">Pulmonary Function Tests and Thoracic Cage Abnormalities</ext-link>," for more information.&#x000a0;FVC is the&#x000a0;maximum volume of air exhaled after a maximal inhalation and depends on lung elasticity, the anatomy of the thoracic cage, and respiratory muscle function. According to the American Thoracic Society (ATS), the TLC predicted (adjusted for gender, age, height) value can be used for assessing the severity of restriction:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>TLC &#x0003c; 80%: Restrictive disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>70-80% predicted: Mild restrictive disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>60-70% predicted: Moderate restrictive&#x000a0;disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>50-60% predicted: Moderately severe restrictive&#x000a0;disease</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>&#x0003c;50% predicted: Severe restrictive&#x000a0;disease&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r21">[21]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>In restrictive diseases, the forced expiratory volume over 1 second (FEV<sub>1</sub>) usually results in a slight decrease or remaining normal, and the ratio of FEV<sub>1</sub> to FVC is generally preserved or increased. These alterations&#x000a0;in pulmonary dynamics can be compensated for by an increased respiratory rate, with hypercapnia developing only at later stages of the disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r22">[22]</xref>&#x000a0;In obstructive diseases,&#x000a0; TLC&#x000a0;and FVC results are&#x000a0;normal (80%-120% of&#x000a0;predicted) or increased, and FEV<sub>1</sub> is reduced compared to&#x000a0;FVC. In summary, the patterns of PFTs in the 2 groups of respiratory diseases are:</p>
        <p>
<bold>Restrictive patterns:&#x000a0;</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased FVC and TLC</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FEV<sub>1</sub>&#x000a0;result slightly decreased or&#x000a0;normal</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC ratio is usually preserved or increased</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reduced&#x000a0;DLCO</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Obstructive patterns:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>TLC&#x000a0;and&#x000a0;FVC results are normal or increased</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Decreased FEV<sub>1&#x000a0;</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC ratio is&#x000a0;decreased</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Reduced&#x000a0;DLCO result in emphysema, normal result in chronic bronchitis, normal or increased result in asthma</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Combined obstructive and restrictive patterns:</bold>
</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Low or normal FVC</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low FEV<sub>1</sub></p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Low FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC ratio</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>TLC decreased, normal, or increased</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>DLCO decreased, normal, or increased</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>Intrinsic interstitial diseases originate from the alveolar capillaries with protein exudation, hemorrhage, and accumulation of inflammatory elements in the alveolar spaces. Alternatively, edema and interstitial inflammatory infiltration may occur, resulting in interstitial fibrosis. Some histopathologic findings are specific to a particular disease process, while others are associated with&#x000a0;multiple conditions.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Usual Interstitial Pneumonia and Idiopathic Pulmonary Fibrosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>The morphological pattern of usual interstitial pneumonia (UIP)&#x000a0;is&#x000a0;characterized by areas of normal lung interspersed with fibrotic areas in a subpleural and paraseptal distribution.&#x000a0;This pattern&#x000a0;is not exclusive to IPF but can be found in rheumatoid arthritis and chronic HP.&#x000a0;IPF is a specific form of chronic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia of unknown etiology (idiopathic UIP),&#x000a0;in which&#x000a0;alveolitis produces fibroblast&#x000a0;proliferation and collagen deposition. One of the&#x000a0;distinctive characteristics of the&#x000a0;UIP/IPF pattern is&#x000a0;the temporal heterogeneity of fibrosis. Dense scar areas of mature collagen fibrosis&#x000a0;and "honeycomb" areas coexist with young fibroblastic proliferations called fibroblastic foci. Furthermore, polyploid proliferation of myofibroblasts, generally&#x000a0;protruding into alveolar or bronchiolar cavities and characterized by a stroma with scarce collagen fibers, can be observed in IPF.&#x000a0;The interstitial septa appear fibrotic and coated with hyperplastic type II pneumocytes or epithelium in bronchiolar metaplasia with a hyperplastic appearance.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Nonspecific Interstitial Pneumonia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia (NSIP) has histologic and radiologic characteristics distinct from other&#x000a0;interstitial diseases&#x000a0;of unknown etiology. Distinguishing between the morphological characteristics is essential because NSIP has a more favorable prognosis than UIP. Morphologically, changes in lung architecture do not fully meet the criteria of other forms of interstitial lung disease.&#x000a0;Three subgroups of the NSIP pattern are recognized:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Cellular pattern:</bold>&#x000a0;Mild to moderate chronic inflammatory&#x000a0;interstitial infiltrate, mainly represented by lymphocytes and plasma cells,&#x000a0;occasionally arranged in nodular aggregates</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Fibrous pattern:</bold> Scarce cellular elements with a greater fibrotic component</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p><bold>Mixed pattern:</bold> Coexistence of&#x000a0;cellular and fibrotic&#x000a0;forms</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
        <p>
<bold>Cryptogenic Organizing Pneumonia</bold>
</p>
        <p>The morphological pattern often shows a bronchiolocentric distribution, preserving the pulmonary architecture in areas surrounding the remodelling process. Morphological characteristics include proliferation of connective tissue (Masson bodies) inside the small airways and alveolar ducts, occlusion of the bronchioles (obliterating bronchiolitis), and surrounding alveoli (organized pneumonia).</p>
        <p>
<bold>Sarcoidosis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Sarcoidosis is a multisystemic inflammatory disease that almost always affects the lungs (90% of cases) with bilateral pulmonary hilar lymph node involvement and&#x000a0;possible simultaneous parenchymal involvement.&#x000a0;Histopathologic features include noncaseating granulomas with tightly packed epithelioid&#x000a0;cells, Langhans giant cells, and T lymphocytes. These findings are localized&#x000a0;in the interstitium adjacent to bronchioles and around and within&#x000a0;vessel walls, pleura, and connective tissue septa. Schaumann bodies (laminated concretions of calcium and protein) and&#x000a0;asteroid elements (stellate inclusions within giant cells) are characteristic. Parenchymal fibrosis in late stages is attributed to intrinsic restriction.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Hypersensitivity Pneumonitis</bold>
</p>
        <p>Hypersensitivity pneumonitis (HP) may&#x000a0;present&#x000a0;as the acute/subacute nonfibrotic or the chronic fibrotic variants. The nonfibrotic type is characterised by&#x000a0;lymphocytic infiltrates centered around bronchioles, extending into the surrounding interstitium, and poorly formed granulomas. The fibrotic HP demonstrates airway-centred fibrosis&#x000a0;with fibroblasts, collagen deposition, and poorly formed granulomas predominantly in subpleural and centriacinar regions. The hallmark of HP is evidence of small airway&#x000a0;disease and interstitial inflammation or fibrosis, depending on the disease stage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r24">[24]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>A comprehensive history and thorough physical examination&#x000a0;are necessary to accurately identify and classify these complex disorders. The severity, onset, duration, and progression of symptoms, as well as the family, smoking, occupational, environmental exposure, and illicit substance histories, are helpful. A history of autoimmune disease can also inform the diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Each particular disease has pathognomonic signs and symptoms that are unique to its pathophysiology. However, most are characterized by an insidious progression of dyspnea.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r19">[19]</xref>&#x000a0;Moreover, ILDs&#x000a0;classically produce the "3 Cs": cough, clubbing of the digits, and coarse crackles on auscultation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients also need to be examined for skin changes, joint swelling or deformity, alopecia, and oral ulcers suggestive of connective tissue diseases. Additional characteristics in patients with extrinsic restriction may include an elevated body mass index, spinal deviation, chest wall abnormality, muscular wasting, abnormal posture, or abdominal distension. Heart failure secondary to chronic restrictive lung disease can present with jugular venous distention&#x000a0;and pedal edema.&#x000a0;Clinical features and prognosis vary among the diseases. For instance, IPF involves a progressive decline in lung function that leads to eventual respiratory failure. Accordingly, the condition has a poor prognosis with a median survival of 3&#x000a0;to 5&#x02009;years after diagnosis.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>The standard evaluation of pulmonary restriction is pulmonary function testing. Initial results indicative of pulmonary restriction include a decreased TLC with a preserved FEV<sub>1</sub>/FVC ratio (greater than 70%). Once a restrictive pattern is suggested on spirometry, patients should be referred for full PFTs with DLCO measurement, which is decreased in patients with intrinsic pulmonary restriction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r26">[26]</xref>&#x000a0;Assessment of residual volume and TLC is&#x000a0;performed by body plethysmography, helium dilution, nitrogen washout, or radiographic methods. Please see StatPearls' companion resources, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/161747">Body Plethysmography</ext-link>" and "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/38845">Physiology, Lung Capacity</ext-link>," for more information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r27">[27]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with extrinsic pulmonary restriction also present with a restrictive pattern on PFTs; however, DLCO remains within the reference range.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;The PFT characteristics of different restrictive etiologies are summarized below in <bold>Table 2.</bold></p>
