        </table-wrap>
        <p>Additional testing with high-resolution computed tomography (HRCT) of the thorax, inflammatory markers, metabolic profile, diagnostic thoracentesis, abdominal ultrasonography, and specific autoantibodies should be performed according to the possible etiology. In IPF, HRCT demonstrates the typical appearance of coarse crosslinking in the basal and posterior regions in the&#x000a0;early stages, and peripheral/subpleural "honeycomb" cystic alterations and bronchiectasis due to traction&#x000a0;fibrosis in advanced disease. A subtype of ILD is idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Guidelines from the ATS suggest considering this diagnosis in adult patients with newly detected&#x000a0;bilateral fibrosis on a chest radiograph&#x000a0;or CT, bibasilar&#x000a0;inspiratory crackles, older&#x000a0;than 60, and lung disease with unknown etiology.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r21">[21]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Management of restrictive lung disease varies depending on the etiology. Patients diagnosed with IPF&#x000a0;were historically treated with immunosuppression; however, antifibrotic drugs such as pirfenidone and nintedanib are increasingly used to&#x000a0;slow disease progression.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with autoimmune conditions associated with ILD, such as systemic sclerosis, are treated with immunosuppressant drugs, including corticosteroids, mycophenolate mofetil, and cyclophosphamide, depending on severity.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;ATS&#x000a0;uses progressive pulmonary fibrosis designation<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;</italic>for non-IPF fibrotic ILDs characterized by symptomatic worsening, an absolute decline in FVC by 5% or more, DLCO by 10% or more, or radiological worsening over 12 months. Objective evidence of progression in fibrotic lung diseases allows for timely revision of therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>Patients with acute exacerbations are usually treated with corticosteroids over&#x000a0;several days; however, prolonged corticosteroid therapy is not recommended due to associated complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r31">[31]</xref>&#x000a0;Oxygen therapy, management of comorbid conditions, and pulmonary rehabilitation, in addition to pirfenidone and nintedanib, are&#x000a0;recommended in IPF.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r32">[32]</xref>&#x000a0;For patients with obesity, management involves weight loss.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r3">[3]</xref>&#x000a0;Tirzepatide is an injectable agent that promotes insulin secretion and sensitization, leading to weight loss comparable to that achieved with bariatric surgery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r33">[33]</xref>&#x000a0;Patients with morbid obesity who do not lose weight with more conservative&#x000a0;methods should be referred for gastric bypass surgery evaluation because it is effective in achieving significant weight loss and improvement in PFTs.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r34">[34]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Concomitant treatment of complications such as obstructive sleep apnea and heart failure should also be pursued while awaiting significant weight reduction.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Patients with neuromuscular diseases should&#x000a0;receive ventilatory support, including positive pressure ventilation, diaphragmatic pacing, or assistance with pneumobelts. Other supportive measures include airway clearance and respiratory muscle training. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/161754">Respiratory Muscle Strength Training</ext-link>," for more information.<bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Chest wall disorders require surgical correction, which is most effective when performed at a young age.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r35">[35]</xref> Patients with extensive pulmonary fibrosis and chronic respiratory failure should be&#x000a0;evaluated for lung transplantation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r36">[36]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>The differential diagnosis of pulmonary restriction remains broad, even after diagnosis is confirmed with PFTs. See <bold>Tables 3 </bold>and<bold> 4</bold> below. Intrinsic pulmonary restriction&#x000a0;may be approached based on the duration of symptoms at presentation.</p>
        <table-wrap id="article-90190.table2" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 3. Differential Diagnosis by Severity</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Acute</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Subacute</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">Chronic</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Acute interstitial pneumonia</p>
<p>Acute hypersensitivity pneumonitis</p>
<p>Drug-induced</p>
<p>Acute eosinophilic pneumonia</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Autoimmune disease&#x02013;associated</p>
<p>Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia</p>
<p>Drug-induced</p>
<p>Subacute hypersensitivity pneumonitis</p>
</td>
                <td rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis&#x000a0;</p>
<p>Nonspecific interstitial pneumonia</p>
<p>Chronic hypersensitivity pneumonitis&#x000a0;</p>
<p>Autoimmune disease&#x02013;associated</p>
<p>Sarcoidosis</p>
<p>Occupational</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
        <table-wrap id="article-90190.table3" position="float" orientation="portrait">
          <caption>
            <title>Table 4. Common Differential Diagnoses of Extrinsic Pulmonary Restriction</title>
          </caption>
          <table style="width: 800px;" border="1" cellpadding="5">
            <tbody>
              <tr style="height: 13px;">
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 141px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Neuromuscular&#x000a0;</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 138px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Skeletal</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 128px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pleural</td>
                <td style="height: 13px; width: 90.7344px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Abdominal/metabolic</td>
              </tr>
              <tr style="height: 131px;">
                <td style="height: 131px; width: 141px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Myasthenia gravis</p>
<p>Muscular dystrophy</p>
<p>Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis&#x000a0;</p>
<p>Phrenic neuropathy</p>
<p>Poliomyelitis</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 131px; width: 138px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Kyphoscoliosis</p>
<p>Pectus excavatum</p>
<p>Costovertebral anomalies</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 131px; width: 128px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Pleural effusion</p>
<p>Pleural fibrosis/plaques</p>
<p>Pleural malignancy</p>
</td>
                <td style="height: 131px; width: 90.7344px;" rowspan="1" colspan="1">
<p>Ascites</p>
<p>Abdominal mass</p>
<p>Obesity</p>
</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </table-wrap>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of restrictive lung disease&#x000a0;varies depending on the etiology. Patients with pulmonary restriction from a pleural effusion often experience resolution of symptoms with drainage of the effusion, and pregnant individuals recover with delivery. Intrinsic pulmonary restriction from nonfibrotic ILDs like cryptogenic organizing pneumonia, cellular NSIP, early-stage sarcoidosis, HP, and autoimmune disease&#x02013;related ILD is associated with excellent long-term outcomes when treated.&#x000a0;Patients diagnosed with IPF have a median survival of 3 to 5 years after diagnosis. Please see StatPearls' companion resource, "<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/point-of-care/20157">Cryptogenic Organizing Pneumonia</ext-link>," for more information.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r37">[37]</xref>&#x000a0;Acute interstitial pneumonia has a grave prognosis, with greater than 70% mortality within 3 months.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r38">[38]</xref><bold>&#x000a0;</bold>Due to the diverse nature of restrictive lung disease, a specific diagnosis should be pursued once pulmonary restriction has been confirmed.&#x000a0;</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Advanced restrictive lung disease results in&#x000a0;hypoxemia, which is initially compensated for by&#x000a0;an increased respiratory rate. The increased&#x000a0;work of breathing can lead to muscle wasting and weight loss. Once compensatory mechanisms fail and hypoxia worsens, patients develop chronic respiratory failure. Sleep disorders, including obstructive sleep apnea, are common in patients with extrinsic pulmonary restriction due to obesity and also occur frequently in intrinsic&#x000a0;restrictive lung disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-90190.r39">[39]</xref>&#x000a0;Chronic respiratory failure and lung architecture distortion lead to pulmonary hypertension and cor pulmonale.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>After identification of the etiology of the pulmonary restriction, patients should be&#x000a0;educated about&#x000a0;the available treatment options. For patients with progressive disease, pulmonary rehabilitation programs offer counseling on breathing techniques and oxygen use, as well as&#x000a0;exercises that can enhance respiratory function and alleviate symptoms. Lifestyle interventions such as exercise, weight loss, and medications can be&#x000a0;effective, especially when obesity is the cause of&#x000a0;pulmonary restriction.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>The management of restrictive lung diseases is often complex and challenging due to the breadth of disease etiologies. For a primary care clinician, knowing when to refer a patient to a pulmonologist can be highly beneficial. Due to the numerous differential diagnoses and potential complications, an interdisciplinary approach is recommended. Apart from the primary care clinician and pulmonologist, the team also includes nurses who periodically assess the patient, as well as internists and intensivists providing care in the inpatient setting.</p>
        <p>Cardiology evaluation should be obtained, especially if pulmonary restriction&#x000a0;leads to signs of&#x000a0;heart strain or failure. Pharmacists are an essential part of the team whenever specialty medications, including antifibrotic agents, are prescribed. Nutritionists should also be included in cases of obesity, and neurologists should be involved in cases of neuromuscular disease. Transplant surgeons may&#x000a0;assess patients with end-stage disease for a lung transplant. Palliative and hospice care specialists should be engaged in the management of terminal disease.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-90190.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
